###### 3.2 *Spacetime, Scalars, Points, Vectors*
(cf. Thorne & Blandford, 2017, ch. 1.2)

*Spacetime* is composed of the familiar 3D Euclidean space (*3-space*) and a universal time *t*. *Points* (= *locations*) in *3-space* are denoted as $\mathcal P$ and $\mathcal Q$. *Points* and *3-space* require *no* coordinates for their definition (Thorne & Blandford, 2017, p.8).

A *scalar* is a single *number*. A *vector* in *3-space* is a *directed straight line segment* connecting *points* $P$ and $Q$. $P$ is the *tail* and $Q$ the *head* of the *arrow* (= *vector*). This kind of *vector* is sometimes denoted as $\Delta \mathbf v = \Delta \mathbf v_{(\mathcal P, \mathcal Q)}$. (Thorne & Blandford, 2017, p.8), 

Equivalently, the *vector* $\Delta \mathbf v$ can be defined as a *direction* $\theta$ at $\mathcal P$ and a *scalar* the vector's *length* $|\Delta \mathbf v|$: 
$\Delta \mathbf v = \Delta \mathbf v _{(\mathcal \theta, |\Delta \mathbf x|)}$.

Often a point $\mathcal O$ is selected as *origin* and is used as a *tail* $\mathcal P = \mathcal O$ in the definition of a *vector*:

$\mathbf v_{\mathcal Q} := \Delta \mathbf v _{(\mathcal P = \mathcal O, \mathcal Q)}$
"

