###### 3.2.1 *Algebraic* Representation of *Points* $\mathcal P_a, Q_a, P_b, Q_b$ and *Vectors* $Δ\mathbf v_{\mathcal P, \mathcal Q}$ in *2D/3D-space* for $Plots.jl$

A *geometric vector* is a *directed straight line* connecting two *points* $P$ and $Q$. $P$ is the *tail* and $Q$ the head of the *arrow* (= *vector*).

The *algebraic* representations of *points* $\mathcal P$ and $\mathcal Q$ suitable for $Plots.jl$ are:

$\mathcal P := (x_{\mathcal P} \in\mathbb R,\ y_{\mathcal P} \in\mathbb R),\; \mathcal Q := (x_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R,\ y_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R)$

of *vector* $Δ\mathbf v_{\mathcal P, \mathcal Q}$:

$Δ\mathbf v_{\mathcal P, \mathcal Q} :=  [\mathcal P, \mathcal Q] = [(x_{\mathcal P} \in\mathbb R,\ y_{\mathcal P} \in\mathbb R),(x_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R,\ y_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R)] =$
$= [tail(Δ\mathbf v_{\mathcal P, \mathcal Q}), head(Δ\mathbf v_{\mathcal P, \mathcal Q})]$

of *vector* $\mathbf v_{\mathcal Q}$:

$\mathbf v_{\mathcal Q} :=  [\mathcal O, \mathcal Q] = [(0.0,...,0.0), (x_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R,\ y_{\mathcal Q} \in\mathbb R)] =$
$= [tail(\mathbf v_{\mathcal Q}), head(\mathbf v_{\mathcal Q})].$
"

