###### 3.3 Geometric Definition of *Dot Product* $\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1 \cdot \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2$
(cf. Walck, 2022, p.134; cf. Thorne & Blandford, 2017, ch. 1.2, p.10)

$\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1 \cdot \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2 = |\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1| |\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2|\ cos\ \theta_{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2}$

where:

$\theta_{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2} = \text{ angle between the } \textit{ tail-to-tail } \text{ aligned vectors } \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2.$

$-1\le\ cos\ \theta_{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2} = \frac{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1 \cdot \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2}{|\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1| |\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2|} \le +1$

The function value $cos\ \theta_{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2}$ can be interpreted as a *measure of similarity* between vectors $\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2$:

$-1\le\ cos\ \theta_{\mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_1, \mathbf \Delta\mathbf v_2} \le +1.$

For appropriately chosen *data* vectors $\mathbf X, \mathbf Y$ function value $cos\ \theta_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y}$ is identical to [*Pearson's correlation coefficient*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearson_correlation_coefficient) $\rho_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y}$:

$-1\le\ cos\ \theta_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y} = \rho_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y} = \frac{\sigma_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y}}{\sigma_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y}} \le +1,$

where:

$\sigma_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y} = cov_{\mathbf X, \mathbf Y}$

$\sigma_{\mathbf X} = \sqrt{var_{\mathbf X}}.$

"

