###### 3.3.10 From *Dot Product* of Vectors $Δ\mathbf v_i, Δ\mathbf v_j$ to *Area* of $Δ\mathbf v_i, Δ\mathbf v_j$-Enclosed *Parallelogram*
(cf. Walck, 2022, p.134; cf. Thorne & Blandford, 2017, ch. 1.2, p.10)

Based on the scalar product $|\mathbf v_i||\mathbf v_j|cos\ θ_{\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v_j}$ of two vectors $\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v_j$, we can calculate the *area* of the *parallelogram* spanned by these vectors. To do this, however, we first have to rotate one vector by 90 degrees and only then we can calculate the *dot product* of these two vectors which equals the *area* of the parallelogram.

*If we first rotate one vector through a right angle in a plane containing the other, and then define the parallelogram, its area is the vector's inner product* (Thorne & Blandford, 2017, p.10)

$|\mathbf v_i||\mathbf v_j|cos\ θ_{\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v_j} \neq |\mathbf v_i||\mathbf v_j|sin\ θ_{\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v_j} = area_{parallelogram}.$

To use the formula of the *dot product* to calculate the *area* of the parallelogram we have to look for an *orthogonal* vector $\mathbf v'_i$ of $\mathbf v_{i; i=1,2}$:

$\mathbf v_i \cdot \mathbf v'_i = 0;\;\; i=1,2$

$|\mathbf v_i||\mathbf v'_j|cos\ θ_{\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v'_j} = |\mathbf v_i||\mathbf v_j|sin\ θ_{\mathbf v_i, \mathbf v_j} = area_{parallelogram}$
"

# ╔═╡ 189b025c-2f31-4556-9cc4-b9048f9be7bb
md"
